<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Libya</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Libya is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast, comprising the historical regions of Tripolitania, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a>. The Fezzan was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire</a>, and the coast was conquered by Arab armies under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>. Libya was a colony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a> from 1911 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and its trade routes linked it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai</a> sultanates.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/libya.jpg" alt="Libya" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Various · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Libya.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…to side with the Ottoman Empire after the outbreak of the First World War, influenced by Pan-Islamic appeals and by the Sanusiyya movement in <b>Libya</b>, was an understandable bid by a client ruler for real independence, but it gave the British the pretext for a swift military expedition.</blockquote><a href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Settled in the habitable depressions of the Fezzan region of modern <b>Libya</b>, above all the Wadi al-Ajal and the Wadi ash-Shati, the Berber-speaking pastoralists who became the Garamantes faced a fundamental constraint: the Sahara's surface water had largely vanished with the end of the Green Sahara period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1938 Governor Italo Balbo oversaw the arrival in <b>Libya</b> of some twenty thousand Italian settlers in a single state-organised convoy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Italo-Turkish War of 1911–1912 brought <b>Libya</b> and the Dodecanese.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Sneferu, first king of the Fourth Dynasty, held territory from ancient <b>Libya</b> in the west to the Sinai Peninsula in the east and to Nubia in the south, and founded a settlement at Buhen in Nubia that lasted two centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…permanent loss of Byzantine power in Syria, Palestine, Egypt and Cyrenaica, and the extension of Arab authority from <b>Libya</b> to the frontiers of Central Asia and the borders of Sind, all within about a quarter of a century by a society with no prior tradition of imperial warfare…</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
